        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 679,
            "height": 541,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "sign says which side of zero each value is on: 1 for positive, -1 for negative, and\n0 for zero itself. The size is thrown away.\n\n-7.5 comes out as -1, 0.001 as 1, 0 as 0. In the demo a sine wave becomes a square\nwave, jumping between 1 and -1 as it crosses zero.\n\nUse it for direction without speed: which way a joint is turning, whether a value is\nabove or below a centre, which way to push. Multiplied by abs of something, it puts\na direction back on a size.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nsign cell by cell (right). Every cell becomes\nfully bright or fully dark.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsign\n\nEXAMPLES:\nsign       which way something is moving, from a velocity\n- 0.5 then sign     above or below the middle of a 0 to 1 control\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe sign of the input: 1, -1 or 0.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nabs     the size without the sign\n>       1 or 0 for above a threshold",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 671,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 525,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "sign says which side of zero each value is on: 1 for positive, -1 for negative, and\n0 for zero itself. The size is thrown away.\n\n-7.5 comes out as -1, 0.001 as 1, 0 as 0. In the demo a sine wave becomes a square\nwave, jumping between 1 and -1 as it crosses zero.\n\nUse it for direction without speed: which way a joint is turning, whether a value is\nabove or below a centre, which way to push. Multiplied by abs of something, it puts\na direction back on a size.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their\nshape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through\nsign cell by cell (right). Every cell becomes\nfully bright or fully dark.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsign\n\nEXAMPLES:\nsign       which way something is moving, from a velocity\n- 0.5 then sign     above or below the middle of a 0 to 1 control\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe sign of the input: 1, -1 or 0.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nabs     the size without the sign\n>       1 or 0 for above a threshold"
        },
